\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Simultaneous Ascending Price Auctions with a Bidding Fee
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 24, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Internet has always been a signifcant tool for establishing markets
 and mechanisms in which buyers and sellers can exchange goods.
 In fact, auctions have become a popular mechanism to exchange items over
 the Internet.
 Over the last several years, there has been a rise in popularity for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

penny auction
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 sites, such as Beezid, Quibids, Madbids, etc.
 With penny auctions, the house auctions a popular and/or expensive item
 starting at $0.00.
 In order to bid on an item, participants need bids, which can be purchased
 from the house.
 Usually, a pack of 100 bids costs about $50, so every bid placed costs
 $.50.
 Each time a bid is placed the price of the item goes up by $0.01.
 The auction usually has a lifespan of about 10 to 20 seconds.
 If any bid is placed during this time, the timer will reset.
 The person who submits the last bid wins the item.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In my experiment, I wish to test the possibility of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

winner's curse,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

buyer's fever
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in this situation, depending on the number of bidders participating in
 the auction, as well as their experience.
 Buyer's fever is a phenomenon in which buyer's submit bid's that are greater
 than an item's true value.
 In the context of the auctions I will be studying, it occurs when the total
 cost of the bids placed by a buyer exceeds the item's value.
 I believe that an increase in the number of bidders will produce more aggressiv
e bidding.
 However, experienced bidders will show sensitivity to the strategic considerati
ons of the auction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Subjects will be recruited for one-hour sessions consisting of a series
 of auction periods in which a single unit of a commodity will be sold each
 period.
 To keep payout amounts reasonable, a conversion rate of 1 cent for every
 2 francs will be instituted.
 Now, in order to participate in the auctions, subjects will be given starting
 balances of 200 francs.
 Before an auction period begins, the value of the item, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

, is drawn randomly from a uniform distribution, and then a public information
 signal affiliated with the actual value of the item to be sold will be
 announced.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Soon after, subjects will have the opportunity to purchase bids where a
 pack of 100 bids costs 50 francs.
 Note that bids not used in the current period can be carried over to future
 periods.
 Then, the actual value of the good is revealed, and the auction begins.
 The initial price of the good is $0.00, and the price goes up by $0.01 each
 time a bid is placed.
 The auction will have a lifespan of 20 second, but the timer will reset
 if any bid is placed.
 The person who submits the last bid when time expires wins the item.
 Profits and and bid purchases are added to the initial balance.
 If a subject's balance becomes negative, the he is no longer permitted
 to bid.
 He will be paid a participation fee, and will be free to leave the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To study the effects of planned variations in the number of bidders, I will
 employ the within-subjects design used by Kagel and Levin (1986) in which
 a set number of active bidders rotates between a larger set of total bidders.
 In each session, I will have a total of 21 subjects who will be randomly
 divided into two groups, one consisting of 7 bidders and the other consisting
 of 14.
 Four active bidders will rotate between the 7 total bidders in the first
 group, and eight active bidders will rotate between the 14 total bidders
 of the second group.
 Finally, for the last few sessions of the experiment, I will randomly recruit
 subjects who did not go bankrupt in the earlier sessions in order to study
 the experience factor.
\end_layout

